WEBVTT

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.920
Stop searching for that
perfect blanket pattern.

00:00:04.160 --> 00:00:05.640
Why not create your own?

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:10.727
Learn how to plan, size,
and customize a blanket on your knitting

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.000
machine using Knit It Now tools.

00:00:13.480 --> 00:00:16.600
No guesswork, just creativity.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:19.720
The first thing to think about is size.

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:24.320
Unlike hand knitting, your knitting
machine has a limited number of needles.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:29.200
We can't just pick up a larger hand
knitting needle and cast on more stitches.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.920
So how do you knit something larger
than your machine's needle bed?

00:00:33.880 --> 00:00:34.920
There's a few options.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:40.240
Number one, knit your blanket
in panels and seam them together.

00:00:41.120 --> 00:00:45.320
This is great for simple patterns
and lets you mix and match colors easily.

00:00:46.240 --> 00:00:49.320
Option number 2, no sew join techniques.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.880
Want to avoid sewing?

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:54.120
Use a sew as you go join.

00:00:55.080 --> 00:01:00.207
Techniques like hairpin lace seams
or a slip stitch crochet can

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:01.920
help you create a seamless look.

00:01:03.640 --> 00:01:06.360
Option number 3, modular knitting.

00:01:06.760 --> 00:01:11.440
You can also build your blanket one
square or one rectangle at a time.

00:01:11.880 --> 00:01:14.200
Maybe even put some triangles together.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:17.720
Think of it like a quilt
but made with yarn.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:21.000
Next, let's talk yarn.

00:01:21.760 --> 00:01:24.880
Now, you don't want to be playing yarn
chicken and run out part of the way

00:01:24.913 --> 00:01:26.440
through your project, right?

00:01:27.640 --> 00:01:29.720
Here's how to estimate
how much you'll need.

00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:33.280
Knit, measure, and weigh a swatch.

00:01:34.120 --> 00:01:37.360
The best way to calculate
yardage is by making a swatch.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:42.240
Measure your gage and then multiply
the total area of your blanket.

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:46.440
Also, factor in the stitch pattern.

00:01:47.120 --> 00:01:49.960
Now, some stitches,
like tuck or slip stitch,

00:01:50.240 --> 00:01:52.560
use more yarn than plain old stockinette.

00:01:53.640 --> 00:01:59.647
If you're using some textures or cables,
plan for even more extra yardage.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.460
Yarns are made for the yarn weight.

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:05.040
Remember that the yarn weight matters.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:09.767
Thicker yarns, like
those you would use on a bulky machine,

00:02:09.800 --> 00:02:12.800
they knit up faster,
but they use more yardage.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:18.607
Lighter yarns on a standard gage machine
may take longer to knit, but they'll

00:02:18.640 --> 00:02:20.440
give you more drape and softness.

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:26.480
Find a yarn you love and look
for blanket patterns using that yarn.

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:29.800
That's the best way to gage
how much yarn you'll need.

00:02:31.520 --> 00:02:34.560
Now for the fun part,
choosing your stitch pattern.

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:40.287
The right stitch pattern can add warmth,
texture, color, and even

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:41.960
bold designs to your blanket.

00:02:43.680 --> 00:02:45.520
Think in terms of simple textures.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:50.607
Tuck and slip stitch makes your blanket
extra cozy without adding

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:51.960
a whole lot of bulk.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.660
It's perfect for baby blankets.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:00.687
If you want to do color work like
fair-isle, remember Fair-Ele can create

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:03.520
long floats in the back of the fabric.

00:03:05.000 --> 00:03:09.727
Lining your blanket with fleece or another
layer of knitting not only will enclosed

00:03:09.760 --> 00:03:13.160
those floats, but it will
make an extra warm blanket.

00:03:13.880 --> 00:03:16.120
Want a blanket that looks
great on both sides?

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.760
Dust off your ribber and try double bed
knitting for a fully reversible design.

00:03:23.240 --> 00:03:24.240
And there you have it.

00:03:24.880 --> 00:03:27.967
By planning your size,
calculating your yarn,

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.847
and choosing the right stitch,
you can create beautiful machine

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:33.640
knit blankets with confidence.

00:03:35.240 --> 00:03:39.407
If you found this video helpful,
check out the blanket classroom at Knit

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:41.880
It Now for even more tips and tutorials.

00:03:42.320 --> 00:03:47.640
We do a deep dive into planning,
designing, and knitting blankets.

00:03:48.640 --> 00:03:54.320
Our DIY blanket pattern is a no-brainer,
no-math blueprint for your creativity.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.000
Happy knitting, and we'll
see you in the next video..

